<script>
import CompanyTable from 'crm/src/companies/lib/CompanyTable.abide'
import companyTableSource from 'crm/src/companies/lib/CompanyTable.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import companyTableTestSource from 'crm/src/companies/lib/CompanyTable.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import ClosingDeals from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/ClosingDeals.abide'
import closingDealsSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/ClosingDeals.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import closingDealsTestSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/ClosingDeals.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import listClosingSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/listClosing.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A table sorts by the header you press">
        <Example
            id="closing-deals"
            title="`SortableTable` — rows sorted by a column, empty values last"
            summary="The open deals and when each is expected to close. Press a header to sort by it, and again to turn the order round."
            serverFiles={['src/pipeline/lib/CLOSE_DATES.server.ts']}
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/ClosingDeals.abide': closingDealsSource,
                'src/pipeline/listClosing.handler.ts': listClosingSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/ClosingDeals.test.ts': closingDealsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ClosingDeals />
        </Example>

        <p>Import it as <code>import SortableTable from '@abide/abide/SortableTable.abide'</code>,
            and the <code>Column</code> and <code>Sort</code> types from <code>@abide/abide</code>.
            Hand it the rows, the columns, and the <code>key</code> each row is told apart by. Bind
            <code>sort</code> to start sorted, or to read the sort back.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>A column's field</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>key</code></td><td>names the column in a <code>Sort</code>, and is the row's field read where there is no <code>value</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>label</code></td><td>the header's text</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>value</code></td><td>a function of the row answering what the column sorts by</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>format</code></td><td>a function of the value and the row answering the cell's text</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>align</code></td><td><code>'start'</code> or <code>'end'</code>, written on the header and each cell</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sortable</code></td><td><code>false</code> for a header with no sort button</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Numbers sort as numbers and dates by time. Text sorts by its words, with the numerals
            in it read as numbers, so deal 9 comes before deal 10. An empty value sorts last in
            both directions, and rows of equal value keep the order they came in. The card's
            stage column sorts by a stage's place in the pipeline through <code>value</code>, and
            shows its name through <code>format</code>.</p>

        <p>A re-sort moves the rows' elements and builds none, keyed by <code>key</code>. The
            card's test asserts it on a full reverse. To sort rows anywhere else, call
            <code>sortRows(rows, columns, sort)</code>, the order the table renders in.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A cell renders a link, or any markup">
        <Example
            id="company-table"
            title="`SortableTable` with `Cell` — each company's name a link to its page"
            summary="Every company the CRM sells to, sortable by name, city or contact. Sort by city, and each name still opens its own company."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyTable.abide': companyTableSource,
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyTable.test.ts': companyTableTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CompanyTable />
        </Example>

        <p>Write a <code>Cell</code> inline component inside the table, and it renders every
            cell's content. It is handed the <code>row</code>, the <code>column</code> and the
            <code>text</code> the cell would show, so a cell you leave alone renders
            <code>text</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Styling a sortable table by its parts and states">
        <p>The table ships no styles and writes no class. Every element you can style carries a
            <code>data-part</code>, and the sorted header carries <code>data-sort</code> beside
            its <code>aria-sort</code>. Any attribute you pass, <code>class</code> included, lands
            on the <code>table</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Part</th><th>States</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>the <code>table</code></td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
                <tr><td>each <code>th</code></td><td><code>header</code></td><td><code>data-sort</code> on the sorted column, <code>"ascending"</code> or <code>"descending"</code>; <code>data-align</code> from the column</td></tr>
                <tr><td>each header's <code>button</code></td><td><code>sort</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                <tr><td>each body <code>tr</code></td><td><code>row</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                <tr><td>each <code>td</code></td><td><code>cell</code></td><td><code>data-align</code> from the column</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>It styles</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'[data-part=header][data-sort=descending] { … }'}</code></td><td>the header sorted descending, from a stylesheet</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="[&_[data-align=end]]:text-right"'}</code></td><td>every header and cell of an end-aligned column</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="[&_[data-sort=ascending]_button]:after:content-[\'_↑\']"'}</code></td><td>an arrow after the sorted header's label</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="[&_[data-part=row]:hover]:bg-ink/5"'}</code></td><td>a row under the pointer</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
